Cajeta — Licensing
==================

Copyright (c) 2026 Julian Klappenbach. All rights reserved.

Cajeta is distributed under a split license. Which terms apply depends on
which part of the project you are using:

  • The COMPILER and TOOLCHAIN are licensed under the
    GNU Affero General Public License, version 3 (AGPLv3).
    Full text: LICENSE-AGPLv3.txt

  • The RUNTIME and STANDARD LIBRARY are licensed under the
    Apache License, version 2.0 (Apache-2.0).
    Full text: LICENSE-Apache-2.0.txt  (and runtime/LICENSE)

This is the same split used by toolchains such as GCC: the compiler is
strongly protected, while the code that ships *inside* the programs you build
is permissive, so your Cajeta programs are never encumbered.


1. What is covered by which license
-----------------------------------

Apache-2.0 (permissive) applies to:
  • runtime/        — the C runtime and the Cajeta standard library
                      (everything that is linked into a compiled Cajeta program)
  • samples/        — example programs, provided so you may freely copy them

AGPLv3 (copyleft) applies to everything else authored in this repository,
including but not limited to:
  • src/            — the Cajeta compiler
  • tools/          — toolchain tools (e.g. the version manager)
  • build-tools/, cmake/, scripts/, packaging/, man/ — build & release tooling
  • docs/, plans/, research/ — project documentation

Third-party components vendored into this repository (for example under
antlr4/ and any other directory carrying its own LICENSE/COPYING file) remain
under their original licenses; nothing here changes those terms.


2. Compiling your own programs does NOT make them AGPL
------------------------------------------------------

The AGPLv3 covers the *source code of the compiler and toolchain itself*. It
does not reach the programs you compile with it:

  • Running the Cajeta compiler over your source code does not place your
    program under the AGPL. The output is yours.
  • The runtime and standard library that get linked into your compiled
    program are licensed under Apache-2.0 (Section 1), which imposes no
    copyleft on your program.

In short: build whatever you like with Cajeta, ship it under any license you
choose. The AGPL obligations attach only if you modify and distribute — or run
as a network service — the compiler/toolchain itself.


3. Dual licensing of the compiler (AGPL or commercial)
------------------------------------------------------

The compiler/toolchain is available under the AGPLv3 *or* a separate
commercial license. If you want to embed, modify, or offer the Cajeta
compiler/toolchain (or a derivative of it) as part of a proprietary product
or a hosted service WITHOUT the obligations of the AGPLv3 — in particular
without releasing your modifications or your service's corresponding source —
you need a commercial license.

For commercial licensing, contact:
    Julian Klappenbach <jklappenbach@gmail.com>


4. Contributions
----------------

Unless you state otherwise, any contribution you intentionally submit for
inclusion in Cajeta is provided under the license that governs the part of the
repository it touches (AGPLv3 for the compiler/toolchain, Apache-2.0 for the
runtime/standard library), and you grant the copyright holder the right to
also license your contribution under the commercial license described in
Section 3. This is what keeps dual licensing possible; substantial
contributions may be asked to sign a Contributor License Agreement (CLA).


5. Trademarks
-------------

This license covers copyright in the code. It does not grant any rights to use
the name "Cajeta", the Cajeta logos, or other Cajeta trademarks, except as
required for reasonable and customary use in describing the origin of the work.


